    "result_text": "The full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `TelephoneNormalizer.java` \u2014 the class that already owns E.164 canonicalization and validity, so the rule lives where it belongs rather than being bolted onto a controller or validator.\n\n**Changes to `TelephoneNormalizer`:**\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` lookup mapping each known country code to its required national-digit count (`\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`), plus a `COUNTRY_CODES` list sorted longest-first so the country code is matched greedily (avoids a shorter code shadowing a longer one).\n- Extended `isValid(...)` to also require `hasValidNationalLength(...)`, a small single-purpose helper that finds the matching country code and checks that the digits after it equal the required national length. Numbers whose country code isn't in the table fall back to the existing generic `MIN_DIGITS`\u2013`MAX_DIGITS` bounds, so unknown countries aren't wrongly rejected.\n- Updated the class Javadoc to document the rule.\n\nBecause `OwnerFieldsValidator` already treats an empty `toE164(...)` result as a rejected `telephone` field error, a wrong-length national number now surfaces as an HTTP 400 with no further wiring needed.\n\nThe new acceptance test (`+61 123` \u2192 400, since `+61` needs 9 national digits) passes, and the valid path still works \u2014 `uniqueTelephone()` produces `04XXXXXXXX`, which drops the leading `0` to 9 national digits under `+61`, exactly the required length.",
